** Verify Docker Version 
  docker -v

** Create a container 
  docker run container_name

** List containers 
  docker ps
  OR
  docker container ps

** List all the currently running docker containers 
  docker ps -a 
  OR
  docker container ps -a

** List all containers that are currently available on te system 
  docker ls -a

** List all images installed on the system 
  docker image ls
  OR 
  docker images

** Delete 1 or more Containers
  1. docker ps -a 
  2. docker rm image_id OR docker container rm image_id
  3. docker ps -a


** Delete image 
  1. docker image ls 
  2. docker image rm container_id OR docker rmi image_id

** Run a container and interact with it 
  docker run -it image_id

** Launch a container and delete automatically 
  docker run -it --rm image_id

** Re-Run a container
  docker start image_id

** Stop a container
  docker stop image_id

** Acces the container and work on it (already started)
  docker exec -it image_id bash 

** Re-launch and interact with teh container in one line command
  docker start -ai image_id

** Mapped Volume
  Definition: 
        Mapped volumes link a explicit directory or file on your host machine directly to a directory inside the container. 
        If you modify a file on your host, the change instantly reflects inside the container, and vice versa
  Command:
        docker run -it --rm local_folder:container_folder image_name

** Managed Volume
  Definition:
        Managed volumes are created and handled entirely by the Docker engine. 
        Docker creates a dedicated directory inside its own internal storage pool and abstractly maps it to your container.
  Commands: 
        Create Volume 
          docker volume create volume_name
        List volume
          docker volume ls
        Delete Volume
          docker volume rm volume_name
        Link a Managed volume
          docker run -it --rm volume_name:container_folder image_name
        
Volume Infos
          docker volume inspect volume_name


A Dockerfile builds a single container image, while a docker-compose.yml file configures and runs multiple interconnected containers as a cohesive system.
  
  



